## 4. Conditional licenses (need verification per port)

> LGPL, MPL, and similar licenses are *conditionally* compatible. Each
> use site must be reviewed.

| License | Condition | Use site(s) | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| LGPL-2.1/3.0 | Dynamic linking OR user-relinkable | `<dep>` in `<module>` | ✅ verified / ⚠️ modify-only-if-allowed |
| MPL-2.0 | File-level copyleft | `<dep>` in `<module>` | ✅ verified |
| BSL without non-commercial clause | Per-license commercial OK | `<dep>` | ⚠️ confirm clause |

### 4.1 LGPL nuances (read carefully)

- LGPL requires **dynamic linking** OR the ability for the end user to
  re-link manually.
- In Go, an LGPL module imported via `import` without modification is OK
  — Go modules are dynamically linked by the linker.
- If the consumer modifies the LGPL source, the consumer must publish the
  modified source AND ensure users can re-link. Document modifications in
  the consumer's port plan artifact (§"Modifications").
- In Odoo, modules under LGPL-3 (the OCA default) can be used as
  dependencies. Same dynamic linking obligation applies.
- **Risk**: a transitive LGPL dep can be contaminated by a GPL/AGPL
  transitive dep — always run `go-licenses` / `pip-licenses` /
  `reuse lint` on the *final* dep tree, not just the top level.

